WEBVTT

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.390
<i>I can't even make a dent...</i>

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.660
<i>This type of tennis exists?!</i>

00:00:12.450 --> 00:00:14.820
<i>This is the world's standard for tennis...</i>

00:00:16.240 --> 00:00:17.870
<i>I have to think of a strategy!</i>

00:01:50.420 --> 00:01:51.500
Huh?

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:52.570
Marcia?!

00:01:54.290 --> 00:01:55.800
I don't get it!

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:59.550
Why are you writing down the details 
of your losing matches so precisely?

00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:00.320
Huh?

00:02:00.320 --> 00:02:03.140
Aren't you frustrated about 
losing against my brother?

00:02:03.140 --> 00:02:06.010
Why do you look so satisfied?!

00:02:06.010 --> 00:02:07.040
Huh?

00:02:07.040 --> 00:02:10.510
Well, your brother's really good.

00:02:10.510 --> 00:02:13.990
So I'm happy I got to play 
against someone good.

00:02:15.070 --> 00:02:17.550
Coach, I'm gonna get more water.

00:02:17.550 --> 00:02:20.150
Okay. You'll be in another match later.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:20.980
Right.

00:02:20.150 --> 00:02:21.830
Eichiro's such a weirdo.

00:02:21.830 --> 00:02:24.580
Not all Japanese people are like that.

00:02:27.600 --> 00:02:31.580
A Vicious Circle is Lucky

00:02:31.950 --> 00:02:34.000
<i>What can I do to win?</i>

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:36.490
Mike!

00:02:36.960 --> 00:02:38.720
Hey, Ei-chan.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.900
I'd like to talk to you about something.

00:02:41.510 --> 00:02:43.850
Is right before dinner okay?

00:02:43.850 --> 00:02:44.720
Sure.

00:02:47.550 --> 00:02:48.890
Hey, Maruo-kun!

00:02:49.340 --> 00:02:53.300
This match is about to end, 
so how about you and I play next?

00:02:53.300 --> 00:02:55.900
No, you should play me instead.

00:02:56.200 --> 00:02:57.700
We heard what's going on.

00:02:57.700 --> 00:03:00.010
If we beat you, we get 
to play against Soji, right?

00:03:00.010 --> 00:03:01.320
Play me, too!

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:02.590
Yeah, me too!

00:03:02.590 --> 00:03:04.140
<i>Talk about popular!</i>

00:03:04.140 --> 00:03:06.410
<i>I mean Ike-kun, of course, not me.</i>

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:08.370
All right, next.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:10.250
Maruo and Krishna, get in.

00:03:13.080 --> 00:03:16.170
After Krishna, I played against another pro

00:03:16.170 --> 00:03:17.760
and ended the day zero for three.

00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:22.380
I realized not only how hard 
these players hit the ball,

00:03:22.610 --> 00:03:25.140
but also that their set-up 
is fast because they go in

00:03:25.140 --> 00:03:26.980
for a score in the second phase.

00:03:26.980 --> 00:03:29.070
Is this the standard in western countries?

00:03:29.070 --> 00:03:30.990
Yeah, that's right.

00:03:30.990 --> 00:03:34.370
Just as I'd expect from you, Ei-chan.
That's an impressive analysis.

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:47.580
3) Go in for the score 
2) If possible, take a shot 
1) Observe

00:03:32.020 --> 00:03:47.580
2) Go in for the score 
1) If possible, take a shot

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:38.550
This is the method that's 
trending in modern-day tennis.

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:44.390
Athletes have gotten faster and 
stronger due to improved physiques.

00:03:44.390 --> 00:03:47.580
So instead of standing up at the 
net where the risk factor is high,

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.200
they learned that it's more efficient 
to hit powerful shots from the baseline

00:03:50.200 --> 00:03:51.710
where the risk factor is low.

00:03:52.580 --> 00:04:05.090
3) Go in for the score 
2) If possible, take a shot 
1) Observe

00:03:52.580 --> 00:04:05.090
2) Go in for the score 
1) If possible, take a shot

00:03:53.240 --> 00:03:56.040
After considering how to fortify 
their decision-making speed

00:03:56.040 --> 00:03:59.230
with minimal risk, the world's
top players determined

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:03.700
that the stage of observing 
through a rally is unnecessary.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:09.510
Of course, this all depends on the player's 
style and the type of court you're on.

00:04:09.510 --> 00:04:13.490
But what do I need to do in order 
to beat this type of tennis?

00:04:13.490 --> 00:04:16.110
You need to build up your speed and power.

00:04:16.110 --> 00:04:20.610
I already knew that aiming from 
the baseline is more efficient.

00:04:20.610 --> 00:04:24.230
I just need the decision-making ability 
to determine how to do that, right?

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:25.450
That's true.

00:04:25.450 --> 00:04:29.520
Then to improve that, instead of 
depending on speed and power,

00:04:29.520 --> 00:04:31.370
would it be all right to focus on control?

00:04:31.600 --> 00:04:33.050
Control?

00:04:33.050 --> 00:04:38.250
Speed and power are guaranteed 
to improve with practice,

00:04:38.250 --> 00:04:41.260
but improving control is difficult.

00:04:41.530 --> 00:04:42.860
It is?

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.510
It varies between people, but normally, yes.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:49.600
I will work on my speed and power, as well,

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:54.520
but I feel like focusing on control 
would be more efficient in my case.

00:04:54.520 --> 00:04:55.870
Hmm...

00:04:55.870 --> 00:04:57.770
I think it would be best for me

00:04:57.770 --> 00:05:00.640
to concentrate mainly on 
an aspect I'm confident in.

00:05:01.620 --> 00:05:03.560
That sounds interesting.

00:05:03.560 --> 00:05:08.290
I'll have the technical advisor come 
up with a training menu for you.

00:05:08.290 --> 00:05:09.980
You can do something like that?

00:05:09.980 --> 00:05:12.540
This is a cutting-edge tennis facility.

00:05:13.130 --> 00:05:17.440
And the staff loves new and interesting ideas.

00:05:17.440 --> 00:05:18.830
Thank you very much.

00:05:25.660 --> 00:05:27.400
So, Alex—

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.550
I'm going to bed. Goodnight.

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.950
I-Is it me, or has he been in a bad mood?

00:05:33.950 --> 00:05:36.810
I heard Ike Soji destroyed him in their match.

00:05:37.500 --> 00:05:40.680
Those two siblings hate losing.

00:05:40.680 --> 00:05:43.320
Best to just stay away from 
them when they're mad.

00:05:43.320 --> 00:05:44.850
You're talking about Alex?

00:05:45.280 --> 00:05:48.530
Out of all of us, he probably 
hates losing the most.

00:05:48.530 --> 00:05:53.330
Krishna, you're always playing it cool, 
and this one gets giddy when he loses.

00:05:53.700 --> 00:05:57.000
Just because he's angry doesn't 
mean he hates losing the most.

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.110
Huh? You think so?

00:05:59.110 --> 00:06:01.820
Regardless, I wish he'd just get over it.

00:06:01.820 --> 00:06:05.150
Well, I'm beat. We should get to sleep, too.

00:06:05.150 --> 00:06:08.010
Oh, I have a few more things to do, so...

00:06:09.370 --> 00:06:12.260
Maruo-kun, you're an honor 
student, aren't you?

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:13.010
Huh?

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:24.990
<i>If I'm hitting from the baseline,
 I need better than 1/64 control,</i>

00:06:24.990 --> 00:06:26.900
<i>and a strong delivery, as well.</i>

00:06:27.520 --> 00:06:29.610
<i>Is there a good way to go about this?</i>

00:06:30.660 --> 00:06:32.950
Florida Exchange Program Day 2

00:06:34.950 --> 00:06:36.450
You didn't sleep well?

00:06:36.720 --> 00:06:38.640
Oh, no. I'm fine.

00:06:38.640 --> 00:06:40.650
I'll wake up once I'm on the court.

00:06:40.650 --> 00:06:44.020
Everyone wants a match against 
you, so don't overdo it.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:45.840
Good morning. Can I sit here, Alex?

00:06:45.840 --> 00:06:47.590
Good morning, Marcia.

00:06:47.590 --> 00:06:50.880
Oh, and Eichiro, Mike said to give this to you.

00:06:51.360 --> 00:06:52.270
This is it!

00:06:52.270 --> 00:06:53.590
Thanks, Marcia.

00:06:54.890 --> 00:06:56.020
Huh?! Why?

00:06:56.020 --> 00:06:56.470
Stop that.

00:06:56.780 --> 00:06:57.910
What is that?

00:06:57.910 --> 00:07:01.590
Mike had the technical advisor come 
up with a training plan for me.

00:07:01.100 --> 00:07:04.610
Training Menu for Improving Control
(Maruo Eiichiro)

00:07:02.340 --> 00:07:05.820
"Training menu for improving control"?

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:07.480
I've never done anything like that.

00:07:07.980 --> 00:07:09.490
What kind of training is it?

00:07:09.490 --> 00:07:13.280
The training is separated 
into five broad parts...

00:07:13.280 --> 00:07:15.060
What? This is...

00:07:15.060 --> 00:07:15.910
What is it?

00:07:16.560 --> 00:07:21.370
The first three are the same kind 
of training I've done in Japan.

00:07:23.990 --> 00:07:26.170
<i>First, image training.</i>

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.770
<i>Divide the court into nine squares, 
and hit each square.</i>

00:07:29.610 --> 00:07:31.840
<i>Second, reconfirm those shots.</i>

00:07:31.840 --> 00:07:33.470
<i>Make sure to take note of</i>

00:07:33.470 --> 00:07:37.350
<i>the sensation of the ball, the path 
it traveled, and where it landed.</i>

00:07:37.350 --> 00:07:42.420
<i>Third, repeat the first two steps while 
keeping my motivation as high as possible.</i>

00:07:43.850 --> 00:07:45.610
<i>1/64!</i>

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.110
<i>Fourth, thoroughly strengthen my lower body.</i>

00:07:49.500 --> 00:07:54.360
<i>Fifth, discover a way to reinforce 
my control by my own means.</i>

00:07:54.790 --> 00:07:56.540
<i>I'll just have to pay attention to my technique</i>

00:07:56.540 --> 00:07:58.370
<i>and figure it out as I go along.</i>

00:07:59.650 --> 00:08:00.870
One-set match.

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.690
Maruo to serve.

00:08:07.500 --> 00:08:10.010
<i>I have to make this two-phase method my own!</i>

00:08:10.010 --> 00:08:12.080
He's struggling again today.

00:08:12.080 --> 00:08:13.770
Game. 3-0.

00:08:13.770 --> 00:08:15.010
Pedro leads.

00:08:15.240 --> 00:08:17.840
<i>Do I have to work on my delivery?</i>

00:08:17.840 --> 00:08:20.640
<i>Or is my control lacking accuracy?</i>

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:22.320
Hey, Maruo.

00:08:22.320 --> 00:08:22.890
Yes?

00:08:23.100 --> 00:08:26.890
I heard that you're training to use 
control as a part of your strategy.

00:08:26.890 --> 00:08:30.920
But you're thinking too much about it 
and looking down at your hands too much.

00:08:30.920 --> 00:08:32.260
Look at your opponent

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:34.660
and at the whole court, not just 
where the ball makes contact.

00:08:34.880 --> 00:08:37.400
<i>The opponent and the court, too...</i>

00:08:37.400 --> 00:08:39.160
<i>Can that be done?</i>

00:08:40.840 --> 00:08:41.660
<i>All right!</i>

00:08:41.870 --> 00:08:43.160
<i>Look at the opponent...</i>

00:08:43.160 --> 00:08:44.540
<i>And the whole court.</i>

00:08:44.820 --> 00:08:45.790
<i>Control!</i>

00:08:46.590 --> 00:08:48.500
<i>I-I looked too far ahead!</i>

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:51.210
What is he doing?

00:08:51.210 --> 00:08:52.790
He's so, well...

00:08:52.790 --> 00:08:54.970
He's clumsy, you know?

00:08:55.830 --> 00:09:00.430
<i>It's hard to believe he only started 
playing at fifteen and got this far...</i>

00:09:01.030 --> 00:09:04.690
<i>It's no good. I need to 
broaden my field of vision.</i>

00:09:06.030 --> 00:09:07.690
<i>Give half of my attention 
to the connection,</i>

00:09:08.060 --> 00:09:11.190
<i>and the other half to my opponent 
and the direction I want to hit...</i>

00:09:14.410 --> 00:09:15.450
<i>Down the line!</i>

00:09:16.450 --> 00:09:18.430
Out. 30-love.

00:09:18.430 --> 00:09:21.110
<i>I could see his movements just now!</i>

00:09:21.110 --> 00:09:23.950
<i>And my control wasn't too shaky.</i>

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:27.210
<i>All right!</i>

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:28.420
<i>One more time!</i>

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:35.720
<i>Strong hits and scoring 
on the second phase.</i>

00:09:35.940 --> 00:09:38.220
<i>Widen my view, and use control!</i>

00:09:40.220 --> 00:09:44.470
Game, set. Winner: Pedro. Score: 6-2.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.980
Yes! Now I can play Ike!

00:09:50.500 --> 00:09:51.700
<i>Little by little...</i>

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:53.100
<i>I'm starting to get it!</i>

00:09:58.660 --> 00:10:02.660
Florida Exchange Program Day 7

00:09:59.640 --> 00:10:02.660
<i>My short stay abroad is 
approaching its second week.</i>

00:10:02.660 --> 00:10:04.160
<i>I'm getting used to the strong hits.</i>

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:08.670
<i>And I've finally acclimated 
to the two-phase method.</i>

00:10:08.990 --> 00:10:11.540
<i>If I can't score on the first shot, 
I try again on the second.</i>

00:10:11.880 --> 00:10:14.170
<i>I'm always in a mindset of 
"defeat or be defeated,"</i>

00:10:14.170 --> 00:10:17.170
<i>so there's no time to think. 
I just let my body react.</i>

00:10:17.170 --> 00:10:19.530
<i>This is what tennis is for me now!</i>

00:10:19.930 --> 00:10:22.190
Whew, another day done.

00:10:22.720 --> 00:10:26.160
Come to think of it, the year's almost over.

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:28.110
Yeah, it'll be New Year's Eve soon.

00:10:27.330 --> 00:10:29.750
Hey, Maruo-kun!

00:10:30.420 --> 00:10:31.350
Ike-kun!

00:10:31.940 --> 00:10:35.140
You're doing well. Mike had 
nothing but praise for you.

00:10:35.140 --> 00:10:37.190
He said you learn fast.

00:10:37.190 --> 00:10:38.690
Really?

00:10:38.690 --> 00:10:40.820
I'm glad to hear that.

00:10:41.880 --> 00:10:42.320
So?

00:10:42.780 --> 00:10:44.600
When are you gonna face me?

00:10:45.520 --> 00:10:46.680
<i>That's right...</i>

00:10:46.680 --> 00:10:51.620
<i>I know I'm adapting to this style of tennis, 
but I still haven't won a match.</i>

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:56.590
I'll be there within the week!

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:00.870
I see, I see.

00:11:02.440 --> 00:11:03.850
I'll be looking forward to it!

00:11:04.130 --> 00:11:04.790
Yeah.

00:11:06.350 --> 00:11:10.350
Florida Exchange Program Day 8

00:11:07.010 --> 00:11:10.350
One-set match. Maruo to serve.

00:11:10.730 --> 00:11:12.580
<i>If I really am getting better,</i>

00:11:12.580 --> 00:11:14.110
<i>I'd like to see proof of it.</i>

00:11:14.680 --> 00:11:17.270
<i>Christopher is fifteen 
and aiming for the pros.</i>

00:11:17.270 --> 00:11:19.540
<i>Last time, I lost 6-2,</i>

00:11:19.540 --> 00:11:21.480
<i>but today, I'm focused on winning.</i>

00:11:22.200 --> 00:11:25.260
<i>I'm going in expecting to beat everyone here.</i>

00:11:25.260 --> 00:11:27.450
<i>No, I will beat them!</i>

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:32.770
<i>I'll deal with the stronger shots from 
the back and use my control to score!</i>

00:11:36.520 --> 00:11:38.610
Oh, he's doing pretty well.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:40.800
He really did improve.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.310
Game: Maruo. 1-0.

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:51.650
<i>Now's my chance!</i>

00:11:53.480 --> 00:11:54.020
Out!

00:11:54.230 --> 00:11:55.630
<i>Damn...</i>

00:11:54.470 --> 00:11:56.820
Game: Christopher. 6-5.

00:12:00.450 --> 00:12:02.650
<i>If I hit it here and tie the score again...</i>

00:12:04.760 --> 00:12:07.940
Game, set. Winner: Christopher.

00:12:07.940 --> 00:12:09.410
Score: 7-5.

00:12:09.410 --> 00:12:10.120
Yes!

00:12:10.670 --> 00:12:13.390
<i>But that was the best match I've had.</i>

00:12:13.390 --> 00:12:14.560
<i>I can do it next time!</i>

00:12:14.560 --> 00:12:16.850
Maruo, you're up with Roger next.

00:12:16.850 --> 00:12:17.630
All right.

00:12:20.390 --> 00:12:22.380
<i>Today I lost all three matches again.</i>

00:12:22.380 --> 00:12:24.130
<i>This makes twenty-four straight losses.</i>

00:12:24.690 --> 00:12:26.640
<i>It'd be one thing if I were 
up against top players,</i>

00:12:26.640 --> 00:12:28.210
<i>but to think I keep losing against players</i>

00:12:28.210 --> 00:12:30.140
<i>who aren't that much better than me...</i>

00:12:30.140 --> 00:12:32.730
Florida Exchange Program Day 9

00:12:31.350 --> 00:12:32.730
<i>I'm going to win today!</i>

00:12:34.770 --> 00:12:37.140
Game, set. Winner: Robbie.

00:12:37.140 --> 00:12:38.860
Score: 6-4.

00:12:39.690 --> 00:12:42.130
Game, set. Winner: Pedro.

00:12:42.130 --> 00:12:43.860
Score: 7-5.

00:12:45.930 --> 00:12:47.660
<i>I'm in good shape today...</i>

00:12:47.660 --> 00:12:50.620
<i>And yet I can't win points 
when I need to the most!</i>

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:52.550
Out!

00:12:52.550 --> 00:12:55.040
Game, set. Winner: Atsushi.

00:12:55.040 --> 00:12:56.290
Score: 6-4.

00:13:00.250 --> 00:13:02.460
<i>This makes twenty-seven straight losses.</i>

00:13:02.930 --> 00:13:03.940
<i>Why?</i>

00:13:08.550 --> 00:13:11.490
<i>I'm able to pull off the new 
techniques I've learned...</i>

00:13:11.490 --> 00:13:13.580
<i>And sometimes I even get close to winning.</i>

00:13:13.580 --> 00:13:15.060
<i>So why can't I win?</i>

00:13:15.060 --> 00:13:16.140
Maruo.

00:13:16.140 --> 00:13:18.190
<i>I feel like I should have at least won a set.</i>

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:18.820
Maruo...

00:13:18.190 --> 00:13:22.220
<i>This 3-5 game, when I failed at control
after getting to 30-all, really hurt.</i>

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:24.110
<i>I'm better prepared to handle risks,</i>

00:13:24.110 --> 00:13:26.910
<i>so why do I keep missing at these crucial times?!</i>

00:13:26.150 --> 00:13:26.910
Maruo.

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:27.830
Yes?!

00:13:28.850 --> 00:13:30.770
I've been calling your name.

00:13:30.770 --> 00:13:31.510
Oh...

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:34.570
You want to be a pro, right?

00:13:34.570 --> 00:13:35.660
Y-Yes.

00:13:35.660 --> 00:13:36.850
Then...

00:13:37.940 --> 00:13:39.420
Believe in yourself.

00:13:42.330 --> 00:13:44.700
<i>Believe in... myself?</i>

00:13:45.780 --> 00:13:48.810
Panicking or getting upset 
won't change a thing.

00:13:49.400 --> 00:13:51.430
Relax and stay positive.

00:13:51.430 --> 00:13:52.750
Oh, right...

00:13:53.930 --> 00:13:55.440
Well, goodnight.

00:13:55.440 --> 00:13:56.690
Goodnight.

00:13:56.920 --> 00:13:58.490
<i>Thanks, Alex.</i>

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:00.110
<i>But...</i>

00:14:02.440 --> 00:14:05.280
<i>It's getting harder to believe in myself.</i>

00:14:07.290 --> 00:14:09.660
<i>I'm confident I can work harder than others.</i>

00:14:10.340 --> 00:14:12.080
<i>But to actually do better than others...</i>

00:14:12.080 --> 00:14:13.660
<i>I never felt like I could do that.</i>

00:14:13.660 --> 00:14:15.910
Game. Winner: Robbie.

00:14:15.910 --> 00:14:17.660
Score: 6-4.

00:14:19.000 --> 00:14:20.350
<i>Why?</i>

00:14:20.350 --> 00:14:22.420
<i>Why can I still not win?</i>

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:23.940
Maruo.

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:25.420
Oh... Yes?

00:14:25.760 --> 00:14:28.660
You're definitely advancing 
and making progress.

00:14:28.660 --> 00:14:31.810
You have my word on that as the chief coach.

00:14:31.810 --> 00:14:35.490
However, right now, you've gotten 
into the habit of losing.

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:36.560
The habit of losing?

00:14:36.560 --> 00:14:39.550
In the moment that determines
whether you win or lose,

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:42.310
your desire to win takes over 
and makes you hit too hard.

00:14:42.530 --> 00:14:45.640
That causes you to lose, 
so you end up hitting even harder.

00:14:45.640 --> 00:14:47.050
It's a vicious cycle.

00:14:47.050 --> 00:14:48.180
No way...

00:14:49.330 --> 00:14:51.210
Head to the monitor room.

00:14:51.210 --> 00:14:52.600
Mike is waiting for you.

00:14:54.580 --> 00:14:56.140
<i>A habit of losing?</i>

00:14:56.140 --> 00:14:58.110
<i>What am I supposed to do about that?</i>

00:14:58.110 --> 00:15:00.040
<i>What does it even mean to 
have a "habit of losing"?</i>

00:15:00.040 --> 00:15:01.130
Oh, Eichiro!

00:15:00.590 --> 00:15:03.420
<i>I know I could just not care about 
winning, but that's impossi—</i>

00:15:02.380 --> 00:15:03.420
Pull yourself together!

00:15:04.650 --> 00:15:06.090
H-Hey!

00:15:07.560 --> 00:15:09.340
Why is Marcia with you?

00:15:09.340 --> 00:15:10.400
I'm escorting him.

00:15:10.400 --> 00:15:11.590
Apparently.

00:15:11.980 --> 00:15:13.780
After looking at your game footage,

00:15:13.780 --> 00:15:16.520
the cause of your losing habit became very clear.

00:15:16.520 --> 00:15:17.730
Losing habit?

00:15:17.730 --> 00:15:18.870
Eichiro has that?

00:15:18.870 --> 00:15:21.100
I feel like I'm about to win, but I never do.

00:15:21.100 --> 00:15:24.030
In order to get used to the 
different style of tennis over here,

00:15:24.030 --> 00:15:28.020
you didn't give winning much thought and 
concentrated on improving your skill.

00:15:28.680 --> 00:15:30.580
As a result, you've quickly gotten used

00:15:30.580 --> 00:15:33.110
to the style, and your technique has improved.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:35.940
But on the flip side,

00:15:35.940 --> 00:15:39.870
your instincts started to lose confidence 
as you lost match after match.

00:15:39.870 --> 00:15:40.580
What?

00:15:40.580 --> 00:15:46.120
So your reason wanted to win 
and started focusing more on that,

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:48.890
but your instincts haven't 
regained their confidence.

00:15:49.440 --> 00:15:51.630
That is the nature of your losing habit.

00:15:51.630 --> 00:15:55.640
So I just have to get my
instincts confident again?

00:15:55.640 --> 00:15:59.390
Yes, basically, you just need 
to believe in yourself.

00:15:59.390 --> 00:16:01.290
Believe in myself?

00:16:01.290 --> 00:16:04.650
Alex said the same thing to me last night.

00:16:06.090 --> 00:16:08.150
Because he's the same way.

00:16:08.690 --> 00:16:12.490
Since he went pro, with his livelihood 
depending on whether he wins or loses,

00:16:12.490 --> 00:16:14.280
he's been on a losing streak.

00:16:14.280 --> 00:16:19.160
I think he feels pressured to provide for 
the family, and that's why he's struggling.

00:16:19.160 --> 00:16:20.560
Alex is going through that?

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:23.570
<i>I didn't know how dire his situation was.</i>

00:16:23.570 --> 00:16:27.200
<i>If I became a pro and played tennis every day</i>

00:16:27.200 --> 00:16:29.850
<i>and continued to lose like this,</i>

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:32.170
<i>I wonder if I'd be able to say such a thing.</i>

00:16:33.270 --> 00:16:34.680
Believe in yourself.

00:16:35.090 --> 00:16:37.300
Alex is amazing.

00:16:37.300 --> 00:16:39.710
But the same goes for all of us.

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:43.200
We all want to be pros, but anyone 
can have a losing streak.

00:16:43.200 --> 00:16:46.240
But in the professional world, 
it's not the people who lose,

00:16:46.240 --> 00:16:49.810
but the people who lose faith in 
themselves that end up dropping out.

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:52.320
That's what Alex tells me all the time.

00:16:52.320 --> 00:16:53.720
Marcia...

00:16:53.720 --> 00:16:59.330
You're not even a pro yet, just like me, 
so stop moping around already!

00:16:59.330 --> 00:17:00.330
Now, now.

00:17:00.950 --> 00:17:02.330
So, Mike...

00:17:02.680 --> 00:17:06.080
What should I do to get my 
instincts to be confident again?

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:10.930
This is a slump that many people 
who badly want to win go through.

00:17:10.930 --> 00:17:14.280
But it's a struggle within yourself, so 
everyone deals with it a different way.

00:17:14.280 --> 00:17:15.590
There's no right answer.

00:17:15.590 --> 00:17:17.090
No right answer?

00:17:17.800 --> 00:17:20.970
I won't tell you to try hard, 
because you're doing so already.

00:17:20.970 --> 00:17:22.600
Instead, I'll tell you this.

00:17:23.540 --> 00:17:25.100
Good luck!

00:17:25.100 --> 00:17:26.850
I'm rooting for you!

00:17:28.690 --> 00:17:30.360
Are you saying it's up to luck?

00:17:31.410 --> 00:17:32.860
Good luck.

00:17:33.430 --> 00:17:36.360
One-set match. Pedro to serve.

00:17:36.360 --> 00:17:39.850
<i>I know what's wrong now, thanks to Mike.</i>

00:17:39.850 --> 00:17:43.370
<i>But what exactly do I do to get 
my instincts confident again?</i>

00:17:48.760 --> 00:17:50.880
Game: Pedro. 1-0.

00:17:52.170 --> 00:17:54.410
Out. Game: Pedro.

00:17:54.410 --> 00:17:55.310
2-0.

00:17:55.310 --> 00:17:56.880
Damn...

00:17:57.250 --> 00:17:59.390
Eichiro's using too much power.

00:17:59.390 --> 00:18:02.140
It's just the first half of the game, 
and he's too worried about winning.

00:18:03.650 --> 00:18:04.600
Whoa!

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:06.900
Are you all right?

00:18:07.740 --> 00:18:08.650
Yeah!

00:18:08.650 --> 00:18:11.140
<i>I didn't even realize they were untied.</i>

00:18:11.140 --> 00:18:14.150
<i>How can I be confident if I 
can't even keep track of myself?</i>

00:18:15.240 --> 00:18:17.350
<i>My strings are messed up, too.</i>

00:18:17.350 --> 00:18:21.830
<i>Judging by how I've started, 
this could be my worst match yet.</i>

00:18:23.910 --> 00:18:25.280
You ready?

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:26.160
Yeah!

00:18:26.160 --> 00:18:27.380
<i>Keep it together!</i>

00:18:27.380 --> 00:18:29.700
<i>If my best isn't enough, 
the rest is up to luck!</i>

00:18:32.390 --> 00:18:33.830
Love-15.

00:18:34.370 --> 00:18:35.130
Huh?

00:18:35.130 --> 00:18:37.170
That one was good.

00:18:37.170 --> 00:18:37.880
Yeah.

00:18:37.880 --> 00:18:39.440
<i>How?</i>

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:41.740
<i>I tripped on my shoelace and fell,</i>

00:18:41.740 --> 00:18:45.690
<i>my strings were messed up, and I returned 
the shot without even having a plan.</i>

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:46.890
<i>Could it be a fluke?</i>

00:18:47.260 --> 00:18:49.030
<i>But there's no other explanation.</i>

00:18:49.030 --> 00:18:51.170
<i>I'll try the same exact thing again.</i>

00:18:51.170 --> 00:18:54.950
<i>I don't have time to fix my shoelaces, 
so I'll fix my strings first.</i>

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:56.900
<i>I also took a deep breath, right?</i>

00:19:00.780 --> 00:19:01.780
Nice return!

00:19:02.530 --> 00:19:04.910
<i>I can go in for the shot on the second phase!</i>

00:19:05.910 --> 00:19:06.910
Yes!

00:19:06.910 --> 00:19:08.160
Love-30.

00:19:08.850 --> 00:19:11.570
<i>I didn't put in too much power that time.</i>

00:19:11.570 --> 00:19:13.100
<i>I have no basis for this,</i>

00:19:13.100 --> 00:19:16.160
<i>but I seem to do well after fixing 
my strings and taking a deep breath.</i>

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:18.920
<i>It's worth trying it again.</i>

00:19:20.150 --> 00:19:20.930
<i>All right!</i>

00:19:23.930 --> 00:19:25.680
15-30.

00:19:26.010 --> 00:19:27.180
30-all.

00:19:28.580 --> 00:19:29.930
40-30.

00:19:30.810 --> 00:19:32.760
Game: Pedro. 3-0.

00:19:32.760 --> 00:19:36.070
<i>I've gotten better, but it's still 
not enough to break his serve.</i>

00:19:38.650 --> 00:19:41.020
Game: Maruo. 3-1.

00:19:41.020 --> 00:19:43.520
<i>At least I was able to hold serve.</i>

00:19:46.770 --> 00:19:48.200
15-love.

00:19:48.200 --> 00:19:49.720
{A: 15 all}15-all.

00:19:49.720 --> 00:19:52.180
Maruo's attitude has changed.

00:19:52.180 --> 00:19:54.470
He was able to hold serve,

00:19:54.470 --> 00:19:56.960
but if he doesn't break Pedro's serve,
he's gonna lose again.

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:58.710
Game: Pedro. 4-1.

00:19:59.060 --> 00:20:00.970
Game: Maruo. 4-2.

00:20:01.210 --> 00:20:03.220
Game: Pedro. 5-2.

00:20:04.510 --> 00:20:06.560
Game: Maruo. 5-3.

00:20:06.970 --> 00:20:09.470
<i>If I don't win the next game, 
I'll lose the match.</i>

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.990
<i>Every point is crucial from here on out.</i>

00:20:11.990 --> 00:20:15.180
<i>And if I don't strike offensively, 
I won't be able to break serve.</i>

00:20:15.180 --> 00:20:17.170
<i>Fixing my strings and taking a deep breath</i>

00:20:17.170 --> 00:20:19.090
<i>does sound kind of superstitious,</i>

00:20:19.090 --> 00:20:21.550
<i>but if it can help me play tennis offensively...</i>

00:20:24.660 --> 00:20:25.240
<i>I'll trust it!</i>

00:20:25.650 --> 00:20:28.670
He's paying a lot of attention to his strings.

00:20:28.670 --> 00:20:29.790
That's it!

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:31.190
It's a routine.

00:20:31.190 --> 00:20:36.250
Routine? You mean when an athlete does 
a certain behavior every time he plays?

00:20:36.250 --> 00:20:37.840
That's right.

00:20:37.840 --> 00:20:40.790
Doing the same thing every time as a routine

00:20:40.790 --> 00:20:45.190
helps maintain a constant state 
of mind and consistent movements.

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:47.570
People often do this while serving.

00:20:47.570 --> 00:20:50.770
They bounce the ball the same 
amount of times each serve.

00:20:52.850 --> 00:20:56.130
When Ei-chan fell before and took up time,

00:20:56.130 --> 00:20:58.430
he might have discovered his own routine.

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:00.270
He fell, but he got lucky?

00:21:00.810 --> 00:21:04.360
Coming up with new ideas in situations 
like that is what Ei-chan does best!

00:21:04.360 --> 00:21:05.910
30-all.

00:21:06.320 --> 00:21:10.160
<i>30-all. This next point is the most important.</i>

00:21:13.960 --> 00:21:15.790
<i>1/64...</i>

00:21:15.790 --> 00:21:16.870
<i>Control!</i>

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:20.420
30-40.

00:21:20.420 --> 00:21:21.480
<i>It went in!</i>

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:22.340
<i>I can do this!</i>

00:21:26.410 --> 00:21:28.320
Game: Maruo. 5-4.

00:21:28.320 --> 00:21:29.460
Pedro leads.

00:21:29.460 --> 00:21:30.650
He broke serve!

00:21:30.650 --> 00:21:33.600
Yeah, he's not using too much power now.

00:21:33.600 --> 00:21:34.850
<i>I wanna keep this up!</i>

00:21:34.850 --> 00:21:38.840
<i>I don't want to change my plan, movements, 
mindset, or anything if I don't have to!</i>

00:21:40.200 --> 00:21:44.060
<i>What's most important now isn't 
doing my best so I can win.</i>

00:21:44.060 --> 00:21:46.610
Game: Maruo. 5-all.

00:21:46.610 --> 00:21:49.540
<i>It's doing what I've always done before:</i>

00:21:49.540 --> 00:21:51.370
<i>believing in myself!</i>

00:21:52.230 --> 00:21:54.340
Game: Maruo. 6-5.

00:21:54.340 --> 00:21:55.290
Maruo leads.

00:21:55.290 --> 00:21:57.000
Maruo broke serve again.

00:22:02.490 --> 00:22:03.500
Game, set.

00:22:03.500 --> 00:22:07.020
Winner: Maruo. Score: 7-5.

00:22:07.020 --> 00:22:07.980
<i>Finally...</i>

00:22:08.570 --> 00:22:11.010
<i>I finally won!</i>

00:22:11.010 --> 00:22:13.010
Ei-chan, nice game!

00:22:13.010 --> 00:22:14.270
Damn it!

00:22:14.270 --> 00:22:16.520
He's finally at the starting line.

00:22:19.950 --> 00:22:21.270
Hey, look at that.

00:22:21.510 --> 00:22:24.090
It's like he won for the first time in his life.

00:22:24.730 --> 00:22:27.280
<i>My first win in America!</i>

00:23:59.950 --> 00:24:24.980
Next Time

00:24:00.220 --> 00:24:02.280
On the day we had our New Year's party,

00:24:02.280 --> 00:24:06.420
I got to talk with Mike, Ike-kun, 
Atsushi-san, and everyone about a lot of things.

00:24:07.130 --> 00:24:10.630
We talked about families, 
significant others, friends...

00:24:10.630 --> 00:24:12.840
And about our favorite tennis athletes.

00:24:12.840 --> 00:24:15.050
We even talked about our future in tennis.

00:24:15.050 --> 00:24:16.800
The next morning, I woke up at five,

00:24:17.260 --> 00:24:20.440
and on the court was my 
first opponent of the year.

00:24:21.000 --> 00:24:24.980
Data for the Future

00:24:21.100 --> 00:24:22.730
On the next episode of <i>Baby Steps</i>:

00:24:22.730 --> 00:24:23.970
"Data for the Future."

00:24:25.170 --> 00:24:31.000
End
